onstruct idx_to_item accordingly.\\n- Replace dictionaries with a CSR sparse matrix for training data: build a sparse matrix of shape (n_users, n_items) and preserve inverse mappings; this will enable scalable operations and closer alignment with the requirement.\\n- Implement a configurable baseline usage: introduce a baseline_mode flag with options like 'global', 'user', 'item', 'none' and adjust prediction accordingly; ensure item-based uses a reasonable baseline (e.g., user mean or global mean depending on mode).\\n- Implement multiple similarity metrics: add cosine similarity computation and an interface to switch between 'pearson' and 'cosine'; add shrinkage parameter to smooth similarities as (n_common / (n_common + shrinkage)) * similarity.\\n- Align prediction formula across modes: ensure item-based predictions use a consistent baseline, ideally the target user's mean when available or a defined global baseline; ensure clipping remains in place.\\n- Add optional coverage metric: report fraction of test interactions for which a prediction was made; currently all tests are filtered to have both user/item in training; consider measuring coverage on full test set including cold-start.\\n- Improve modularity: structure into modules: data_loader (with  ml-100k/1m support), preprocessor (mapping, train/test split, CSR construction), cf_model (training, neighbor selection, prediction), evaluator (RMSE/MAE/coverage); add a simple CLI or API for experiments.\\n- Extend dataset support and reliability: handle ml-100k and ml-1m consistently, and handle network failures gracefully; provide deterministic fallbacks when datasets are unavailable.\\n- Add unit tests or example notebooks to validate each component (loading, mapping, similarity, predictions) and run at least two variants to gather comparable results.\\n\\nNotes on the current results: The reported RMSE is 0.947 and MAE is 0.740 on whatever filtered test set was used; given the incorrect item mapping and limited modularity, this should be treated as a baseline/demonstration rather than a reliable experiment.", is_satisfactory=False)\
